# Line endings are decided here, not by each contributor's core.autocrlf.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, correctness depends on local git config.
# The repository's blobs are LF today only because contributors happen to run
# core.autocrlf=true; a contributor with autocrlf=false on Windows would commit
# CRLF, and a CRLF shebang (`#!/bin/bash\r`) makes Linux report
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory". These rules make the outcome
# the same on every machine regardless of how git is configured locally.
#
# `text` means "store LF in the repository". `eol=` controls only what lands in
# the working tree on checkout — so these rules do not rewrite existing blobs.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts: LF everywhere, including a Windows working tree. A CR in the
# shebang line breaks the script on Linux, macOS and WSL.
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Windows-only batch wrappers: CRLF in the working tree. cmd.exe tolerates LF
# for simple scripts but is unreliable with it around labels, goto, and
# parenthesized if-blocks — and these files never run anywhere else.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# PowerShell parses LF correctly on both Windows PowerShell 5.1 and pwsh.
*.ps1  text eol=lf

# Binary — never convert. None are tracked today; these guard future additions,
# because a single CRLF substitution inside a binary corrupts it silently.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary

